// examples/quant/implied_vol_surface — the raw SVI volatility surface and the
// closed-form no-arbitrage conditions on it.
//
// Copyright 2025 STARGA Inc.
// Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
// Part of the MIND project (Machine Intelligence Native Design).
//
// HOW THIS DIFFERS FROM `examples/quant/implied_vol`, STATED UP FRONT.  The two
// modules share a name and almost nothing else, and conflating them is the
// obvious mistake a reader makes, so:
//
//   * `examples/quant/implied_vol` inverts ONE price at ONE strike and ONE
//     maturity into ONE number.  Its subject is the ITERATION: a fixed-count
//     bracketed root-find, and the argument that its trip count cannot depend on
//     a datum.  It says nothing about whether the resulting vols, taken
//     together, are a coherent surface.
//
//   * THIS module never inverts anything as its primary job.  It takes a
//     PARAMETRIC SMILE — five numbers that describe total implied variance
//     across all strikes at one maturity — and asks the question a single
//     inversion cannot: is the surface these numbers describe ARBITRAGE-FREE?
//     A desk can invert a thousand quotes flawlessly and still end up with a
//     surface that implies a negative risk-neutral density, which is a
//     free-money machine for whoever trades against it.
//
// The two are complementary and deliberately kept apart.  The BS round-trip KAT
// at the bottom of this file is the single place they touch: it prices an option
// at an SVI vol and recovers that vol with a solver of the `implied_vol` shape,
// which is what ties the parametric surface back to a tradeable price.
//
// THE MODEL.  Raw SVI (Gatheral 2004), in TOTAL IMPLIED VARIANCE:
//
//     w(k) = a + b * ( rho*(k - m) + sqrt((k - m)^2 + sigma^2) )
//
// where k = log(K/F) is log-moneyness and w(k) = sigma_BS(k)^2 * T.  Five
// parameters: a (vertical level), b (wing slope scale), rho (skew/rotation, in
// (-1,1)), m (horizontal translation), sigma (curvature at the vertex; NOT a
// volatility, despite the name the literature uses — hence `p_sigma` in the
// signatures below, to keep it visibly distinct from a Black-Scholes sigma).
//
// WHY TOTAL VARIANCE AND NOT VOL.  Both arbitrage conditions are natural in w
// and unnatural in sigma_BS.  Calendar-spread arbitrage is exactly "w must be
// non-decreasing in T at fixed k"; in vol terms that is "sigma^2*T must be
// non-decreasing", which is the same statement wearing a disguise that invites
// the error of testing sigma instead of sigma^2*T.  Butterfly arbitrage is the
// Durrleman condition, which is a polynomial in w, w' and w''.  Parameterising
// in w means neither condition needs a conversion step where a sign can be lost.
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT A LIBRARY.  Raw SVI is published, standard, and nothing here
// is a contribution to finance.  A production surface would fit the five
// parameters to quotes, use SSVI or eSSVI to enforce no-calendar-arbitrage
// across slices by construction, and clamp into Gatheral & Jacquier's (2014)
// sufficient-condition region.  None of that is here.  The contribution is that
// w, w', w'', the Durrleman function g, and the Black-Scholes round-trip that
// anchors them are all built from `+ - * /` and a re-derived sqrt/exp/log/erfc
// on the strict scalar path, so a surface declared arbitrage-free on x86 is
// bit-for-bit the same surface declared arbitrage-free on ARM.
//
// WHY THAT MATTERS MORE HERE THAN FOR A PRICE.  A price is a number that two
// systems can disagree about in the last bit without anyone caring.  `g(k) >= 0`
// is a BOOLEAN.  When g is genuinely near zero — which is exactly where a fitted
// surface sits, because a well-calibrated fit presses right up against the
// no-arbitrage boundary — the last bit of g decides whether the risk system says
// "tradeable" or "reject".  Two substrates that differ by one ulp in g at that
// boundary produce two different regulatory answers from the same inputs, and
// there is no reconciliation report that can explain it.  Determinism converts
// that from an irreproducible incident into a property.
//
// ANALYTIC DERIVATIVES, NOT FINITE DIFFERENCES.  w' and w'' below are the exact
// derivatives of the closed form.  A bumped-and-revalued w'' would carry two
// subtractions of nearly-equal numbers AND a step-size choice, and the step size
// would become a second thing g depends on.  Since g is a sign test, a
// step-size-dependent g is a step-size-dependent arbitrage verdict.
//
//     w'(k)  = b * ( rho + (k-m)/sqrt((k-m)^2 + sigma^2) )
//     w''(k) = b * sigma^2 / ((k-m)^2 + sigma^2)^(3/2)
//
// Note w'' > 0 everywhere for b > 0, sigma > 0: raw SVI is convex in k by
// construction.  Convexity is NOT sufficient for no butterfly arbitrage, which
// is precisely why g has to be computed rather than assumed — see the VIOLATING
// parameter set in the KAT, which is convex everywhere and still admits
// butterflies.
//
// THE libm NAME-CAPTURE HAZARD.  Every kernel below is named `dm_*` or `det_*`,
// and every SVI entry point `svi_*`.  A MIND function whose name matches a C99
// libm symbol (exp, log, pow, sin, cos, erf, erfc) can be captured by LLVM and
// constant-folded to the HOST libm when its argument is a compile-time constant,
// silently skipping the MIND body — which would substitute a non-deterministic
// host routine for the deterministic one on exactly the inputs a KAT uses.  The
// prefix discipline is load-bearing, not cosmetic.  Do not rename these.
//
// SELF-CONTAINED, AND WHY.  The detmath kernels and the Black-Scholes pricer are
// duplicated below instead of imported.  mindc 0.10.2 cannot carry an f64 across
// a module boundary (roadmap 17.1): an imported `fn f(x: f64) -> f64` types as
// `(i64) -> i64` at the call site and miscompiles silently.  This mirrors
// `examples/detmath_kat`, `examples/quant/black_scholes` and
// `examples/quant/implied_vol`.  The kernel block is copied BYTE-IDENTICALLY
// from `examples/quant/black_scholes/main.mind` so the kernels cannot silently
// diverge between examples; a diff of the two blocks must come back empty.
// deferred: replace the duplicated kernel block with `import std.detmath;` once
// roadmap 17.1 lands — upgrade path: delete the block between the KERNEL BEGIN
// and KERNEL END markers and call the std surface directly.
//
// NO `[test]` FUNCTIONS, AND WHY.  The mindc 0.10.2 `[test]` interpreter
// mis-evaluates an f64 comparison in a function body (it takes the then-branch
// unconditionally).  Every guarded function here would return through its
// leading `if x != x` guard and every interpreted assertion would pass
// vacuously — a false green.  That failure is especially poisonous for this
// module: the entire deliverable is a family of SIGN TESTS on g, and an
// interpreter that takes every then-branch would report every surface as
// arbitrage-free, including the one constructed below specifically to violate.
// The native backend evaluates f64 branches correctly, so the gate is
// build-and-run: `mindc run`, exit code 0 means every check passed.  The count
// of failures is the exit code, so a partial failure is never rounded to
// success.
//
// REFERENCES (original papers only):
//   Gatheral, J. (2004). "A Parsimonious Arbitrage-Free Implied Volatility
//     Parameterization with Application to the Valuation of Volatility
//     Derivatives." Presentation, Global Derivatives & Risk Management, Madrid.
//     (The raw SVI form used here.)
//   Durrleman, V. (2010). "From Implied to Spot Volatilities." Finance and
//     Stochastics 14(2), 157-177.  (The g(k) >= 0 butterfly condition.)
//   Gatheral, J. & Jacquier, A. (2014). "Arbitrage-Free SVI Volatility
//     Surfaces." Quantitative Finance 14(1), 59-71.  (Necessary and sufficient
//     conditions; the SSVI family that removes calendar arbitrage by
//     construction.)
//   Lee, R. W. (2004). "The Moment Formula for Implied Volatility at Extreme
//     Strikes." Mathematical Finance 14(3), 469-480.  (Why the wings must be
//     linear in k and bounded by 2 — the asymptotic KAT below.)
//   Black, F. & Scholes, M. (1973). "The Pricing of Options and Corporate
//     Liabilities." Journal of Political Economy 81(3), 637-654.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// =========================== KERNEL BEGIN ==================================
// Duplicated verbatim from std/detmath.mind (roadmap 17.1 blocks importing).
// Do not "improve" these: every polynomial is a fixed left-fold Horner
// recurrence in source order.  Reassociating one changes the last bit of every
// price downstream and breaks the cross-substrate hash.

fn absf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 0.0 - x;
    }
    return x;
}

// +inf by byte-identical IEEE overflow; never a synthesised NaN.
fn inf() -> f64 {
    let mut v: f64 = 1.0;
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i < 350 {
        v = v * 10.0;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// 0x7FF8000000000000 — the MIND-canonical quiet NaN, pinned so an
// out-of-domain result is byte-identical (x86 sets the sign bit, ARM does not).
fn canonical_qnan() -> f64 {
    return __mind_bits_to_f64(9221120237041090560);
}

fn rnd_to_i64(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if x >= 0.0 {
        return (x + 0.5) as i64;
    }
    return 0 - ((0.5 - x) as i64);
}

fn det_exp(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let ln2_hi: f64 = 0.6931467056274414;
    let ln2_lo: f64 = 0.00000047493250390941725;
    let inv_ln2: f64 = 1.4426950408889634;
    let k: i64 = rnd_to_i64(x * inv_ln2);
    let kf: f64 = k as f64;
    let r: f64 = (x - kf * ln2_hi) - kf * ln2_lo;
    let mut t: f64 = 0.0000000000007647163731819816;
    t = 0.00000000016059043836821613 + r * t;
    t = 0.00000000208767569878681 + r * t;
    t = 0.00000002505210838544172 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000002755731922398589 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000027557319223985893 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000248015873015873 + r * t;
    t = 0.0001984126984126984 + r * t;
    t = 0.001388888888888889 + r * t;
    t = 0.008333333333333333 + r * t;
    t = 0.041666666666666664 + r * t;
    t = 0.16666666666666666 + r * t;
    t = 0.5 + r * t;
    t = 1.0 + r * t;
    t = 1.0 + r * t;
    let mut y: f64 = t * 1.0;
    let mut j: i64 = 0;
    if k >= 0 {
        while j < k {
            y = y * 2.0;
            j = j + 1;
        }
    } else {
        while j < (0 - k) {
            y = y / 2.0;
            j = j + 1;
        }
    }
    return y;
}

fn dm_exp(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x >= 709.7827128933841 {
        return inf();
    }
    if x <= 0.0 - 745.1332191019412 {
        return 0.0;
    }
    return det_exp(x);
}

fn det_log_core(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let ln2: f64 = 0.6931471805599453;
    let mut k: i64 = 0;
    // f64-param-init: seed a loop-carried f64 binding via the exact identity
    // `x * 1.0` so mindc 0.10.2 does not infer it as i64.
    let mut m: f64 = x * 1.0;
    while m >= 1.4142135623730951 {
        m = m / 2.0;
        k = k + 1;
    }
    while m < 0.7071067811865476 {
        m = m * 2.0;
        k = k - 1;
    }
    let s: f64 = (m - 1.0) / (m + 1.0);
    let s2: f64 = s * s;
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 29;
    while n >= 1 {
        acc = 1.0 / (n as f64) + s2 * acc;
        n = n - 2;
    }
    return (k as f64) * ln2 + 2.0 * s * acc;
}

fn dm_log(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 0.0;
    }
    if x == 0.0 {
        return 0.0 - inf();
    }
    if x >= inf() {
        return inf();
    }
    return det_log_core(x);
}

fn dm_sqrt(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    return sqrt(x);
}

// erf by its Maclaurin series, ascending fixed left-fold.  Certified |x| < 2.
fn erf_series(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let two_over_sqrtpi: f64 = 1.1283791670955126;
    let x2: f64 = x * x;
    let mut t: f64 = x * 1.0;
    let mut s: f64 = x * 1.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 1;
    while n < 60 {
        t = t * (0.0 - x2) / (n as f64);
        s = s + t / ((2 * n + 1) as f64);
        n = n + 1;
    }
    return two_over_sqrtpi * s;
}

// erfc by a fixed-depth backward continued fraction.  Certified x >= 1.
// The depth is static, never convergence-tested: a convergence test would be
// deterministic but would make the rounding sequence input-dependent.
fn erfc_cf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let sqrtpi: f64 = 1.7724538509055159;
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 200;
    while n >= 1 {
        acc = ((n as f64) * 0.5) / (x + acc);
        n = n - 1;
    }
    return det_exp(0.0 - x * x) / sqrtpi / (x + acc);
}

fn dm_erfc(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x >= inf() {
        return 0.0;
    }
    if x <= 0.0 - inf() {
        return 2.0;
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 2.0 - dm_erfc(0.0 - x);
    }
    if x < 1.0 {
        return 1.0 - erf_series(x);
    }
    return erfc_cf(x);
}

// Standard normal CDF.  Computed as 0.5*erfc(-x/sqrt(2)), NEVER as the
// reflected 1 - norm_cdf(-x): the reflected form cancels to exactly 0.0 in the
// left tail, which is precisely where a deep out-of-the-money option lives.
// See the deep-OTM KAT below, where the true call premium is 4.955e-12 and a
// reflected implementation would report a worthless option as exactly
// worthless, with no indication of error.
fn dm_norm_cdf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    let inv_sqrt2: f64 = 0.7071067811865476;
    return 0.5 * dm_erfc(0.0 - x * inv_sqrt2);
}

// Standard normal PDF, phi(x) = exp(-x^2/2)/sqrt(2*pi).  Needed by gamma,
// vega and theta.  1/sqrt(2*pi) is a rounded constant, not a division.
fn dm_norm_pdf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    let inv_sqrt_2pi: f64 = 0.3989422804014327;
    return inv_sqrt_2pi * det_exp(0.0 - 0.5 * x * x);
}

// ============================ KERNEL END ===================================

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Black-Scholes-Merton, re-declared here as the ANCHOR of the round-trip KAT.
//
// Copied verbatim from `examples/quant/black_scholes` (roadmap 17.1: an f64
// that crossed a module boundary would be silently miscompiled).  Only the
// four functions the round-trip needs are carried over: `bs_valid`, `bs_d1`,
// `bs_call` and `bs_vega`.  This file does not re-export a second public
// definition of a Black-Scholes price; these stay private so there is nothing
// here that could drift from the sibling example's public surface.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn bs_valid(s: f64, k: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> i64 {
    if s != s {
        return 0;
    }
    if k != k {
        return 0;
    }
    if sigma != sigma {
        return 0;
    }
    if t != t {
        return 0;
    }
    if s <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if k <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if sigma <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if t <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

fn bs_d1(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    let sq: f64 = sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    return (dm_log(s / k) + (r - q + 0.5 * sigma * sigma) * t) / sq;
}

fn bs_d2(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    return bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t) - sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
}

fn bs_call(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    let d2: f64 = d1 - sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    let df_r: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - r * t);
    let df_q: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - q * t);
    return s * df_q * dm_norm_cdf(d1) - k * df_r * dm_norm_cdf(d2);
}

fn bs_vega(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if bs_valid(s, k, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let sqt: f64 = dm_sqrt(t);
    let d1: f64 = bs_d1(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    return s * dm_exp(0.0 - q * t) * dm_norm_pdf(d1) * sqt;
}

// ===========================================================================
// THE SVI SURFACE.
//
// PARAMETER ADMISSIBILITY.  Every entry point below returns the canonical qNaN
// on an inadmissible parameter set rather than a plausible-looking number.  The
// conditions are not stylistic:
//
//   b >= 0        — b is the wing scale.  A negative b inverts both wings, so
//                   total variance DECREASES without bound as |k| grows and the
//                   surface has no lower bound at all.  b = 0 is admitted: it
//                   is the flat slice w = a, which is degenerate but not
//                   arbitrageable, and refusing it would refuse the zero-skew
//                   limit that a fit legitimately approaches.
//   |rho| < 1     — STRICT.  At |rho| = 1 one wing of w is flat and the other
//                   has slope 2b, which is Lee's (2004) moment-formula boundary:
//                   the surface is at the edge where the underlying's second
//                   moment ceases to exist.  Strict rejection also keeps
//                   sqrt(1 - rho^2) in `svi_k_min` away from a divide by zero.
//   p_sigma > 0   — STRICT.  p_sigma = 0 collapses the hyperbola to its two
//                   asymptotes joined at a corner: w'' is a Dirac spike at
//                   k = m, and the Durrleman function is undefined there rather
//                   than merely large.
//   a + b*p_sigma*sqrt(1-rho^2) >= 0
//                 — the MINIMUM of w must be non-negative.  This is the one
//                   that is easy to omit, because `a >= 0` looks like the
//                   obvious level condition and is neither necessary nor
//                   sufficient: a can be negative if the wings lift the vertex
//                   back above zero, and a positive a can still give negative
//                   variance if b*rho pulls it down.  Negative total variance
//                   is not a near-miss; it is an imaginary volatility, and
//                   `svi_implied_vol` would hand back a NaN from sqrt() with no
//                   indication of which parameter caused it.
//
// The last condition is checked HERE, at the parameter level, exactly so the
// failure is attributed to the parameters rather than surfacing later as a NaN
// from an unrelated-looking sqrt.
// ===========================================================================

// The minimum of w, the closed form.  Setting w'(k) = 0 gives
//     rho + (k-m)/sqrt((k-m)^2 + sigma^2) = 0
//  => (k-m)/sqrt((k-m)^2 + sigma^2) = -rho
//  => k = m - rho*sigma/sqrt(1 - rho^2)
// and the minimum value is w(k_min) = a + b*sigma*sqrt(1 - rho^2).  Both are
// exact; neither is a search.  `svi_k_min` is asserted against w'(k_min) == 0
// in the KAT, which is the cheapest possible check that w' is really the
// derivative of w.
fn svi_k_min_raw(rho: f64, m: f64, p_sigma: f64) -> f64 {
    return m - rho * p_sigma / dm_sqrt(1.0 - rho * rho);
}

fn svi_w_min_raw(a: f64, b: f64, rho: f64, p_sigma: f64) -> f64 {
    return a + b * p_sigma * dm_sqrt(1.0 - rho * rho);
}

fn svi_params_valid(a: f64, b: f64, rho: f64, m: f64, p_sigma: f64) -> i64 {
    if a != a {
        return 0;
    }
    if b != b {
        return 0;
    }
    if rho != rho {
        return 0;
    }
    if m != m {
        return 0;
    }
    if p_sigma != p_sigma {
        return 0;
    }
    if b < 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if rho <= 0.0 - 1.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if rho >= 1.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if p_sigma <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    // Non-negative minimum total variance.  See the block comment above for why
    // `a >= 0` is the wrong test.
    if svi_w_min_raw(a, b, rho, p_sigma) < 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

// --- w(k): total implied variance -------------------------------------------
//     w(k) = a + b*( rho*(k-m) + sqrt((k-m)^2 + p_sigma^2) )
// EVALUATION ORDER IS PART OF THE CONTRACT.  The inner bracket is summed left
// to right before the single multiplication by b.  Distributing b across the
// bracket is algebraically identical and numerically different, and here that
// difference lands directly on a sign test (see g below), not merely on a
// displayed value.  Do not "simplify" this expression.
pub fn svi_total_variance(k: f64, a: f64, b: f64, rho: f64, m: f64, p_sigma: f64) -> f64 {
    if svi_params_valid(a, b, rho, m, p_sigma) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if k != k {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let km: f64 = k - m;
    return a + b * (rho * km + dm_sqrt(km * km + p_sigma * p_sigma));
}

// --- w'(k): the exact first derivative ---------------------------------------
//     w'(k) = b*( rho + (k-m)/sqrt((k-m)^2 + p_sigma^2) )
// The quotient is bounded in (-1, 1), so w' is bounded in (b*(rho-1),
// b*(rho+1)) — the two wing slopes asserted in the KAT.
pub fn svi_w_prime(k: f64, a: f64, b: f64, rho: f64, m: f64, p_sigma: f64) -> f64 {
    if svi_params_valid(a, b, rho, m, p_sigma) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if k != k {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let km: f64 = k - m;
    let d: f64 = dm_sqrt(km * km + p_sigma * p_sigma);
    return b * (rho + km / d);
}

// --- w''(k): the exact second derivative -------------------------------------
//     w''(k) = b*p_sigma^2 / ((k-m)^2 + p_sigma^2)^(3/2)
// Written as `d*d*d` from a single sqrt rather than as a pow(.,1.5): pow is one
// of the libm names that LLVM can capture (see the header), and a cubed sqrt is
// both deterministic and cheaper.  Strictly positive for b > 0 — raw SVI is
// convex in k always.  Convexity is NOT no-arbitrage; see the VIOLATING set.
pub fn svi_w_prime2(k: f64, a: f64, b: f64, rho: f64, m: f64, p_sigma: f64) -> f64 {
    if svi_params_valid(a, b, rho, m, p_sigma) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if k != k {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let km: f64 = k - m;
    let d: f64 = dm_sqrt(km * km + p_sigma * p_sigma);
    return b * p_sigma * p_sigma / (d * d * d);
}

// --- Black-Scholes implied vol implied BY the surface ------------------------
//     sigma_BS(k, t) = sqrt(w(k) / t)
// This is a DEFINITION, not an inversion: it is the vol that, plugged into
// Black-Scholes at maturity t, reproduces the total variance the surface
// declares.  The KAT asserts `svi_implied_vol(...)^2 * t == svi_total_variance`
// as an exact-ish consistency check, and separately round-trips it through a
// real price and a real solver — the second is the one that could fail if this
// definition were wrong in a way squaring would hide.
pub fn svi_implied_vol(k: f64, t: f64, a: f64, b: f64, rho: f64, m: f64, p_sigma: f64) -> f64 {
    if svi_params_valid(a, b, rho, m, p_sigma) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if t != t {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if t <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let w: f64 = svi_total_variance(k, a, b, rho, m, p_sigma);
    if w != w {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    return dm_sqrt(w / t);
}

// The location of the minimum, public because a caller checking a fit wants it.
pub fn svi_k_min(a: f64, b: f64, rho: f64, m: f64, p_sigma: f64) -> f64 {
    if svi_params_valid(a, b, rho, m, p_sigma) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    return svi_k_min_raw(rho, m, p_sigma);
}

// ===========================================================================
// NO-BUTTERFLY ARBITRAGE: the Durrleman condition.
//
//     g(k) = ( 1 - k*w'(k)/(2*w(k)) )^2
//            - ( w'(k)^2 / 4 ) * ( 1/w(k) + 1/4 )
//            + w''(k)/2
//
// g(k) >= 0 for all k is equivalent to the risk-neutral density implied by the
// slice being non-negative, i.e. to the absence of butterfly arbitrage.  It is
// not an approximation and not a sufficient-condition heuristic: g is, up to a
// strictly positive factor, the implied density itself.
//
// WHY EACH TERM MATTERS, since the temptation is always to drop one:
//
//   * The `(1 - k*w'/(2w))^2` term is the only one that is positive by
//     construction and the only one carrying k explicitly.  Dropping it makes
//     g negative nearly everywhere and the check fires constantly — a loud
//     failure, therefore the safe one to get wrong.
//
//   * The `-(w'^2/4)(1/w + 1/4)` term is the destabiliser: it is the one that
//     drives g negative on a steep slice.  Dropping it makes g >= 0 almost
//     always, which reads as "our surfaces are always clean" — a SILENT
//     failure, and the dangerous one.
//
//   * The `+w''/2` term is the convexity rescue.  Since w'' > 0 always for raw
//     SVI, dropping it makes g strictly smaller everywhere, so the check
//     becomes conservative — it rejects surfaces that are actually fine.  That
//     is the mutation used to verify this gate can go red, because it is the
//     only one whose direction is unambiguous.
//
// A HARD LIMITATION, STATED PLAINLY.  This function evaluates g AT A POINT.
// "g(k) >= 0 for all k" is a statement over the whole real line, and no finite
// grid can establish it.  A pointwise scan can only ever FALSIFY no-arbitrage,
// never certify it: a violation narrower than the grid spacing is invisible.
// Certification needs the Gatheral-Jacquier (2014) parameter-domain conditions,
// which are algebraic in (a,b,rho,m,sigma) and not implemented here.  The KAT
// below is therefore honest about what it proves — it demonstrates that the
// detector FIRES on a known-bad slice and stays quiet on a known-good one, not
// that any slice is arbitrage-free.
// ===========================================================================
pub fn svi_durrleman_g(k: f64, a: f64, b: f64, rho: f64, m: f64, p_sigma: f64) -> f64 {
    if svi_params_valid(a, b, rho, m, p_sigma) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if k != k {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let w: f64 = svi_total_variance(k, a, b, rho, m, p_sigma);
    // w == 0 makes 1/w infinite.  It can only happen when a + b*p_sigma*
    // sqrt(1-rho^2) == 0 exactly, i.e. on the boundary `svi_params_valid`
    // admits.  Refuse rather than return an infinity that would compare
    // "greater than zero" and be read as arbitrage-free.
    if w <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let w1: f64 = svi_w_prime(k, a, b, rho, m, p_sigma);
    let w2: f64 = svi_w_prime2(k, a, b, rho, m, p_sigma);
    let t1: f64 = 1.0 - k * w1 / (2.0 * w);
    let t2: f64 = (w1 * w1 / 4.0) * (1.0 / w + 0.25);
    return t1 * t1 - t2 + w2 / 2.0;
}

// Scan g over a FIXED grid and return 1 if no violation was found, 0 if one
// was.  The trip count is a compile-time constant derived from `steps`, which
// every call site passes as a literal: there is no convergence test and no
// early exit, so both arms of the loop do the same work and the number of
// iterations cannot depend on a datum.
//
// The loop runs to completion even after a violation is found.  An early
// `return 0` would make the number of floating-point operations depend on WHERE
// the violation sits, which is a datum-dependent trip count wearing a different
// hat.  The cost is a handful of wasted evaluations; the benefit is that the
// instruction trace of a scan is a function of `steps` alone.
//
// Returns 0 (violation) if g is NaN anywhere, which is the fail-closed reading:
// a surface whose arbitrage condition could not be evaluated is not a surface
// that has been shown to be free of arbitrage.
pub fn svi_butterfly_ok(k_lo: f64, k_hi: f64, steps: i64, a: f64, b: f64, rho: f64, m: f64, p_sigma: f64) -> i64 {
    if svi_params_valid(a, b, rho, m, p_sigma) == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if steps < 1 {
        return 0;
    }
    if k_lo != k_lo {
        return 0;
    }
    if k_hi != k_hi {
        return 0;
    }
    let mut ok: i64 = 1;
    let span: f64 = k_hi - k_lo;
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i <= steps {
        let frac: f64 = (i as f64) / (steps as f64);
        let k: f64 = k_lo + span * frac;
        let g: f64 = svi_durrleman_g(k, a, b, rho, m, p_sigma);
        if g != g {
            ok = 0;
        } else {
            if g < 0.0 {
                ok = 0;
            }
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return ok;
}

// ===========================================================================
// NO-CALENDAR-SPREAD ARBITRAGE.
//
// Total implied variance must be NON-DECREASING in maturity at fixed
// log-moneyness:
//     w(k, T2) >= w(k, T1)   for all k, whenever T2 > T1.
//
// The economics: a calendar spread is long the far-dated option and short the
// near-dated one at the same strike.  If total variance fell with maturity, that
// spread would have a negative price for a payoff that is non-negative in every
// state — a riskless profit, taken by anyone who notices.
//
// WHY THIS IS TESTED ON w AND NOT ON sigma.  Implied VOL routinely decreases
// with maturity (an inverted term structure is ordinary and not arbitrageable);
// implied VARIANCE times maturity must not.  Testing sigma instead of sigma^2*T
// would reject every inverted term structure as arbitrage and pass some genuine
// violations.  This is the single most common error in a hand-rolled surface
// check, and it is the reason this module parameterises in w.
//
// Note the asymmetry with the butterfly check, which is deliberate: the
// butterfly condition constrains ONE slice, so it needs g.  The calendar
// condition relates TWO slices, so it needs two parameter sets and a
// maturity-ordering check.  A module that only implements g has checked half
// the problem and will happily certify a surface that is arbitrageable across
// maturities.
//
// Same limitation as the butterfly scan, stated with the same plainness: this
// is a pointwise scan over a fixed grid.  It can falsify, not certify.
// ===========================================================================
pub fn svi_calendar_ok(k_lo: f64, k_hi: f64, steps: i64, t1: f64, a1: f64, b1: f64, rho1: f64, m1: f64, s1: f64, t2: f64, a2: f64, b2: f64, rho2: f64, m2: f64, s2: f64) -> i64 {
    if svi_params_valid(a1, b1, rho1, m1, s1) == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if svi_params_valid(a2, b2, rho2, m2, s2) == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if t1 != t1 {
        return 0;
    }
    if t2 != t2 {
        return 0;
    }
    if t1 <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    // The near slice must genuinely be nearer.  Handing the two slices over in
    // the wrong order would otherwise "prove" no arbitrage by testing the
    // inequality backwards, which is the failure mode a caller cannot see.
    if t2 <= t1 {
        return 0;
    }
    if steps < 1 {
        return 0;
    }
    let mut ok: i64 = 1;
    let span: f64 = k_hi - k_lo;
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i <= steps {
        let frac: f64 = (i as f64) / (steps as f64);
        let k: f64 = k_lo + span * frac;
        let w1: f64 = svi_total_variance(k, a1, b1, rho1, m1, s1);
        let w2: f64 = svi_total_variance(k, a2, b2, rho2, m2, s2);
        if w1 != w1 {
            ok = 0;
        } else {
            if w2 != w2 {
                ok = 0;
            } else {
                if w2 < w1 {
                    ok = 0;
                }
            }
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return ok;
}

// ===========================================================================
// THE ROUND-TRIP SOLVER — fixed iteration count, bracketed.
//
// Present only to close the loop between the parametric surface and a real
// price: price an option at the SVI vol, then recover that vol from the price.
// The full argument for the design lives in `examples/quant/implied_vol`; the
// part that must be restated here because it is a HARD CONSTRAINT:
//
//   the trip count is a compile-time CONSTANT (60), never a convergence test.
//
// A tolerance loop — `while absf(f) > tol { ... }` — runs a number of times
// decided by a floating-point comparison.  On a substrate where `absf(f)` lands
// one ulp on the other side of `tol`, the loop runs one more time or one fewer
// and returns a different number.  The divergence is not bounded by an ulp,
// because the extra step moves the iterate; and it is unrecoverable after the
// fact, since "how many iterations did the other machine take" is not
// deducible from the answer.  A fixed count costs wasted arithmetic and buys a
// trip count no input can influence.  That trade is the whole point.
//
// The bracket invariant is what keeps a fixed count correct: [lo, hi] contains
// the root at entry and at every step, because the Black-Scholes price is
// strictly increasing in sigma, so the sign test can only discard the half that
// cannot contain it.  A Newton step is accepted only when it lands STRICTLY
// inside the current bracket; otherwise the step is discarded and the iteration
// bisects.  Branch choice is data-dependent; iteration count is not.
// ===========================================================================
fn rt_iters() -> i64 {
    return 60;
}

fn rt_solve_call(price: f64, s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if price != price {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if price <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if t != t {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if t <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if s <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if k <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let p_lo: f64 = bs_call(s, k, r, q, 0.0001, t);
    let p_hi: f64 = bs_call(s, k, r, q, 5.0, t);
    if p_lo != p_lo {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if p_hi != p_hi {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    // A price outside [p(lo), p(hi)] has no root in the bracket, and bisection
    // on a rootless bracket converges confidently to an endpoint.  That is the
    // most dangerous failure a root-find has, because the answer looks
    // converged.  Refuse it.
    if price < p_lo {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if price > p_hi {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    // f64-param-init: seed each loop-carried f64 binding via `x * 1.0`.
    let mut lo: f64 = 0.0001 * 1.0;
    let mut hi: f64 = 5.0 * 1.0;
    let mut sigma: f64 = 0.5 * (lo + hi);
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i < rt_iters() {
        let f: f64 = bs_call(s, k, r, q, sigma, t) - price;
        if f > 0.0 {
            hi = sigma;
        } else {
            lo = sigma;
        }
        let v: f64 = bs_vega(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
        let mut cand: f64 = 0.5 * (lo + hi);
        if v > 0.0 {
            let n: f64 = sigma - f / v;
            if n > lo {
                if n < hi {
                    cand = n;
                }
            }
        }
        sigma = cand;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return sigma;
}

// ===========================================================================
// KNOWN-ANSWER TESTS.
//
// THE ORACLE.  Every reference value below was produced by an INDEPENDENT
// 50-digit oracle — mpmath 1.3.0 at `mp.dps = 50`, evaluating w, w', w'', g and
// the Black-Scholes call in 50-digit arithmetic and rounded ONCE to f64.  None
// was produced by this implementation, and none was recalled from memory.  A
// KAT generated from the code it tests proves only that the code is unchanged,
// not that it is correct.
//
// THE TWO PARAMETER SETS, AND WHY BOTH ARE REQUIRED:
//
//   FREE   a=0.04  b=0.10  rho=-0.30  m=0.0  p_sigma=0.20
//          A typical index slice: modest level, gentle negative skew, wide
//          vertex.  g ranges 0.49 .. 1.25 over k in [-0.5, 0.5] — comfortably
//          positive, nowhere near the boundary.
//
//   VIOL   a=0.01  b=0.90  rho=-0.75  m=0.1  p_sigma=0.05
//          A butterfly-ARBITRAGEABLE slice: large b and strongly negative rho
//          give a left wing of slope b*(1-rho) = 1.575, which is above Lee's
//          moment-formula bound of 2 only in the limit but is already steep
//          enough, against a total variance of 0.178 at the money, to drive g
//          negative.  Oracle: g(0.0) = -1.406, g(-0.25) = -0.787,
//          g(-0.5) = -0.448.  Note it is still CONVEX everywhere (w'' > 0 by
//          construction), which is exactly why convexity cannot substitute for
//          the Durrleman condition.
//
// A NO-ARBITRAGE CHECK THAT NEVER FIRES IS NOT A CHECK.  The FREE/VIOL pair is
// the single most valuable thing in this file.  Asserting only that a good
// surface passes is satisfied by `return 1;`.  Asserting that a bad surface is
// caught is satisfied by `return 0;`.  Only the pair together says the detector
// discriminates, and each half of the pair is a positive control for the other.
//
// FIVE CLASSES OF CHECK, deliberately:
//   1. STRUCTURAL IDENTITIES — butterfly +/-, calendar +/-, wing asymptotics,
//      w'(k_min) == 0, vol/variance consistency.  These hold regardless of the
//      oracle and therefore survive a mistyped reference, which at this level
//      of algebra is the likeliest error of all.  For this module they are not
//      a supplement to the value checks; they ARE the deliverable.
//   2. ROUND-TRIP THROUGH A PRICE — price at the SVI vol, recover it with the
//      fixed-count solver.  Ties the parametric surface to something tradeable.
//   3. VALUE CHECKS — against the 50-digit oracle, within a stated ulp band.
//   4. DOMAIN — every inadmissible parameter set returns the canonical qNaN.
//   5. ADVERSARIAL — a deliberately WRONG reference must be REJECTED, counted
//      POSITIVELY into `fails`.  Without these the bands prove nothing.
//
// WHAT THIS KAT CANNOT CATCH, STATED PLAINLY.  Three things, and none of them
// is fixable by tightening a band:
//
//   (a) The scans are POINTWISE over a finite grid.  `svi_butterfly_ok`
//       returning 1 does NOT mean the slice is arbitrage-free; it means no
//       violation was found at 41 points.  A violation narrower than the grid
//       spacing is invisible.  Certification requires the Gatheral-Jacquier
//       parameter-domain conditions, which are not implemented here.
//
//   (b) The value bands are tolerances, so they are structurally blind to any
//       change that perturbs a result by less than the band — including a
//       reassociation that is algebraically identical and numerically
//       different.  Tightening them is not the fix: they must stay wide enough
//       to absorb the honest difference between a re-derived sqrt/erfc and a
//       50-digit oracle.
//
//   (c) A tolerance band is ALSO blind to a change in the solver's iteration
//       count, because the iteration has converged well inside the band by
//       step 60.  Changing `rt_iters` from 60 to 50 would not redden a single
//       check here.  That is not a defect in the test; it is precisely why the
//       count must be a source-level constant a reader can see, rather than a
//       property a band could certify.
//
// Detecting (b) or (c) requires a BIT-EXACT reference, which is the job of the
// cross-substrate CI fixture (tests/cross_substrate_identity.rs): it compares
// hashes across x86 `avx2` and ARM `neon`, and a hash has no tolerance to hide
// in.  This KAT proves the surface algebra is CORRECT; the fixture proves it is
// REPRODUCIBLE.  A green KAT is not evidence of byte-identity.
// ===========================================================================

fn chk_ulp(got: f64, want: f64, ulps: f64) -> i64 {
    let eps: f64 = 0.0000000000000002220446049250313;
    let tol: f64 = ulps * eps * absf(want);
    if absf(got - want) <= tol {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn chk_abs(got: f64, want: f64, abstol: f64) -> i64 {
    if absf(got - want) <= abstol {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn is_nan(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if x != x {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// `cond` must be 1.  Returns 1 on failure so it can be added into `fails`.
fn expect_true(cond: i64) -> i64 {
    if cond == 1 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

// `cond` must be 0.  The negative-control form: used wherever the claim is
// "this check FIRES", which is the half of a detector that is never exercised
// by a happy-path test.
fn expect_false(cond: i64) -> i64 {
    if cond == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

fn gt_zero(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if x > 0.0 {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn lt_zero(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

pub fn main() -> i64 {
    let mut fails: i64 = 0;

    // FREE slice — arbitrage-free.
    let fa: f64 = 0.04;
    let fb: f64 = 0.1;
    let frho: f64 = 0.0 - 0.3;
    let fm: f64 = 0.0;
    let fs: f64 = 0.2;

    // VIOL slice — butterfly-arbitrageable.  Admissible as PARAMETERS (b >= 0,
    // |rho| < 1, p_sigma > 0, min variance 0.0397 > 0), which is the point: the
    // parameter guard cannot see butterfly arbitrage, only g can.
    let va: f64 = 0.01;
    let vb: f64 = 0.9;
    let vrho: f64 = 0.0 - 0.75;
    let vm: f64 = 0.1;
    let vs: f64 = 0.05;

    // =======================================================================
    // 1. STRUCTURAL IDENTITIES.
    // =======================================================================

    // --- NO-BUTTERFLY, POSITIVE HALF: the FREE slice must pass --------------
    // Oracle g over k in [-0.5, 0.5]: min 0.4925 at k = -0.5, max 1.2462 at
    // k = 0.  Asserted pointwise at the five oracle points AND as a 41-point
    // scan, because the scan is the function a caller actually uses and it has
    // its own loop that could be wrong independently of g.
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(svi_durrleman_g(0.0 - 0.5, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs)));
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(svi_durrleman_g(0.0 - 0.25, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs)));
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(svi_durrleman_g(0.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs)));
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(svi_durrleman_g(0.25, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs)));
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(svi_durrleman_g(0.5, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs)));
    fails = fails + expect_true(svi_butterfly_ok(0.0 - 0.5, 0.5, 40, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs));
    // Wider window: the FREE slice is clean well beyond the quoted strikes too.
    fails = fails + expect_true(svi_butterfly_ok(0.0 - 1.5, 1.5, 60, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs));

    // --- NO-BUTTERFLY, NEGATIVE HALF: the VIOL slice must be CAUGHT ---------
    // This is the half that makes the half above mean something.  A detector
    // that returns 1 unconditionally passes every check in the positive block
    // and fails every check here.
    // Oracle: g(0.0) = -1.4061, g(-0.25) = -0.7872, g(-0.5) = -0.4475.
    fails = fails + expect_true(lt_zero(svi_durrleman_g(0.0, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs)));
    fails = fails + expect_true(lt_zero(svi_durrleman_g(0.0 - 0.25, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs)));
    fails = fails + expect_true(lt_zero(svi_durrleman_g(0.0 - 0.5, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs)));
    fails = fails + expect_false(svi_butterfly_ok(0.0 - 0.5, 0.5, 40, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs));
    // ... and the VIOL slice's PARAMETERS are perfectly admissible, so the
    // violation is genuinely invisible to the parameter guard.  If this check
    // ever fails, the negative control above has become vacuous: it would be
    // passing because the parameters were rejected, not because g fired.
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(svi_total_variance(0.0, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs)));
    fails = fails + is_nan(svi_total_variance(0.0, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs));
    fails = fails + is_nan(svi_durrleman_g(0.0, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs));
    // The VIOL slice is CONVEX everywhere (w'' > 0), which is why a convexity
    // test would certify it as fine.  Assert the convexity, so the claim that
    // convexity is insufficient is demonstrated rather than merely asserted in
    // a comment.
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(svi_w_prime2(0.0, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs)));
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(svi_w_prime2(0.0 - 0.5, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs)));
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(svi_w_prime2(0.5, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs)));
    // Part of the VIOL slice IS clean — g(0.25) = +0.442.  Assert it, so the
    // negative control is known to be catching a LOCAL violation rather than a
    // function that returns negative everywhere.
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(svi_durrleman_g(0.25, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs)));

    // --- CALENDAR SPREAD, POSITIVE HALF -------------------------------------
    // Near slice T1 = 1.0 with the FREE parameters; far slice T2 = 2.0 with a
    // higher level (a = 0.06) and slightly wider wings.  Oracle: the far-minus-
    // near difference in w is between +0.0242 and +0.0266 over k in [-0.5, 0.5]
    // — strictly positive everywhere, so non-decreasing holds with room.
    let ga: f64 = 0.06;
    let gb: f64 = 0.11;
    let grho: f64 = 0.0 - 0.28;
    let gm: f64 = 0.0;
    let gs: f64 = 0.22;
    fails = fails + expect_true(svi_calendar_ok(0.0 - 0.5, 0.5, 40, 1.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs, 2.0, ga, gb, grho, gm, gs));

    // --- CALENDAR SPREAD, NEGATIVE HALF: a violating pair must be CAUGHT ----
    // Same shape, LOWER level at the longer maturity (a = 0.02 against 0.04).
    // Oracle: w_far - w_near = -0.02 at every k, uniformly.  Total variance
    // falling with maturity is exactly the calendar-spread arbitrage.
    let ba: f64 = 0.02;
    fails = fails + expect_false(svi_calendar_ok(0.0 - 0.5, 0.5, 40, 1.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs, 2.0, ba, fb, frho, fm, fs));
    // The violating far slice is itself a perfectly valid, butterfly-free
    // slice — the arbitrage is purely between the two maturities.  Asserting
    // this keeps the negative control from passing for the wrong reason (e.g.
    // because the far parameters were rejected outright).
    fails = fails + expect_true(svi_butterfly_ok(0.0 - 0.5, 0.5, 40, ba, fb, frho, fm, fs));
    // Maturity ORDER is checked, not assumed: handing the slices over backwards
    // must be refused rather than silently testing the inequality the wrong way.
    fails = fails + expect_false(svi_calendar_ok(0.0 - 0.5, 0.5, 40, 2.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs, 1.0, ga, gb, grho, gm, gs));
    // Equal maturities are also refused: "non-decreasing between T and T" is
    // vacuously true and would let a caller certify a surface it never tested.
    fails = fails + expect_false(svi_calendar_ok(0.0 - 0.5, 0.5, 40, 1.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs, 1.0, ga, gb, grho, gm, gs));
    // Direct pointwise confirmation of both directions, independent of the scan
    // function, so a bug in the scan's loop cannot hide a bug in the ordering.
    let w_near: f64 = svi_total_variance(0.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs);
    let w_far_ok: f64 = svi_total_variance(0.0, ga, gb, grho, gm, gs);
    let w_far_bad: f64 = svi_total_variance(0.0, ba, fb, frho, fm, fs);
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(w_far_ok - w_near));
    fails = fails + expect_true(lt_zero(w_far_bad - w_near));

    // --- WING ASYMPTOTICS ---------------------------------------------------
    // As k -> +inf, w'(k) -> b*(1+rho); as k -> -inf, w'(k) -> b*(1-rho).  Both
    // follow from (k-m)/sqrt((k-m)^2 + p_sigma^2) -> +/-1.  For the FREE slice
    // those limits are 0.07 and 0.13.
    //
    // This identity is the sharpest available check on the SIGN of rho.  A
    // flipped rho leaves w(0) nearly unchanged (it shifts by 2*b*rho*m, which
    // is ZERO at m = 0) and leaves w'' completely unchanged, so a value check
    // at the money would not see it at all.  It swaps the two wing slopes, and
    // only this check notices.
    //
    // The convergence is algebraic, not exponential: at k = 200 the oracle
    // gives w'(200) = 0.0699999500000375, i.e. 5e-8 short of 0.07.  The band is
    // stated as an ABSOLUTE 1e-6 rather than in ulps for exactly that reason —
    // an ulp band would be asserting that a finite k is infinity, which is
    // false.  1e-6 is 20x the true residual at k = 200, wide enough to be
    // honest and narrow enough that a swapped wing (0.07 against 0.13, a gap of
    // 0.06) is rejected by five orders of magnitude.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(svi_w_prime(200.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.07, 0.000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(svi_w_prime(0.0 - 200.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.0 - 0.13, 0.000001));
    // Same limits, one order of magnitude closer in, to show the approach is
    // monotone rather than a coincidence at one point.  Oracle at k = 50:
    // 0.06999920000959987 and -0.12999920000959989.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(svi_w_prime(50.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.07, 0.00001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(svi_w_prime(0.0 - 50.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.0 - 0.13, 0.00001));
    // w itself must GROW LINEARLY, not merely have a bounded slope.  Oracle:
    // w(200) = 14.0400099999975, w(-200) = 26.0400099999975.  A surface whose
    // w saturated would satisfy the slope limits nowhere and this check
    // everywhere it was wrong; together they pin the wing shape.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_total_variance(200.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 14.0400099999975, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_total_variance(0.0 - 200.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 26.0400099999975, 1024.0));
    // The wing slopes must also be OPPOSITE in sign about the vertex — a slice
    // whose w' had the same sign on both sides would be monotone, not a smile.
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(svi_w_prime(200.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs)));
    fails = fails + expect_true(lt_zero(svi_w_prime(0.0 - 200.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs)));

    // --- MINIMUM: w'(k_min) must be exactly zero ----------------------------
    //     k_min = m - rho*p_sigma/sqrt(1 - rho^2)
    // The cheapest possible check that w' really is the derivative of w: the
    // closed-form stationary point is derived from w' = 0, so if w' were the
    // derivative of something else, this would not vanish.  The oracle at 50
    // digits gives exactly 0 at k_min, and the f64 evaluation reproduces it to
    // better than 1e-17, so an absolute band is used (a relative one against a
    // target of zero is undefined).
    let kmin: f64 = svi_k_min(fa, fb, frho, fm, fs);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(kmin, 0.0628970902033151, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(svi_w_prime(kmin, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.0, 0.0000000000001));
    // ... and it is a MINIMUM, not a maximum or a saddle: w'' > 0 there, and w
    // is strictly larger on both sides.  Asserting only w' == 0 would be
    // satisfied by a w' that is identically zero.
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(svi_w_prime2(kmin, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs)));
    let w_at_min: f64 = svi_total_variance(kmin, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(w_at_min, 0.05907878402833891, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(svi_total_variance(kmin + 0.1, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs) - w_at_min));
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(svi_total_variance(kmin - 0.1, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs) - w_at_min));
    // The VIOL slice has a minimum too, at a different place.  Checking both
    // stops `svi_k_min` from passing by returning a constant that happens to
    // suit the FREE parameters.
    let kmin_v: f64 = svi_k_min(va, vb, vrho, vm, vs);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(kmin_v, 0.1566946709513841, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(svi_w_prime(kmin_v, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs), 0.0, 0.0000000000001));

    // --- CONSISTENCY: iv^2 * t == w -----------------------------------------
    // `svi_implied_vol` is sqrt(w/t) by definition, so squaring it and
    // multiplying by t must return w to within the rounding of one sqrt and one
    // square — a few ulps, not a tolerance.  Checked at three maturities and
    // three log-moneyness points so a t- or k-dependent error cannot hide.
    let ivs: f64 = svi_implied_vol(0.0 - 0.3, 0.5, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(ivs * ivs * 0.5, svi_total_variance(0.0 - 0.3, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 16.0));
    let ivm: f64 = svi_implied_vol(0.0, 1.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(ivm * ivm * 1.0, svi_total_variance(0.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 16.0));
    let ivl: f64 = svi_implied_vol(0.3, 2.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(ivl * ivl * 2.0, svi_total_variance(0.3, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 16.0));
    // The term structure of VOL falls here (0.3464 at T=0.5 down to 0.1732 at
    // T=2) even though total VARIANCE rises with T by construction.  This is
    // the inverted-term-structure case that a naive sigma-based calendar check
    // would flag as arbitrage.  Asserting it makes the w-versus-sigma argument
    // in the calendar block a demonstrated fact rather than a claim.
    let iv_t05: f64 = svi_implied_vol(0.0, 0.5, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs);
    let iv_t20: f64 = svi_implied_vol(0.0, 2.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs);
    fails = fails + expect_true(lt_zero(iv_t20 - iv_t05));

    // =======================================================================
    // 2. ROUND-TRIP THROUGH A BLACK-SCHOLES PRICE.
    //
    // Price a European call at S = 100, r = q = 0, T = 1, strike K = S*e^k, with
    // the vol the surface declares at that k.  Then recover the implied vol from
    // that price with the fixed-count solver and assert it returns the SVI vol.
    //
    // This is the only place the parametric surface touches a tradeable
    // quantity.  Without it, every check above could pass on a surface whose
    // "vol" was an internally consistent number that no option was ever priced
    // at.  With r = q = 0 the forward equals the spot, so k = log(K/S) is
    // exactly the log-moneyness the surface is parameterised in — using a
    // non-zero rate without discounting the strike would silently shift k and
    // the round-trip would recover a vol from the wrong point on the smile.
    //
    // Bands are stated per case and track 1/vega, as in
    // `examples/quant/implied_vol`: the at-the-money case is best-conditioned,
    // the out-of-the-money case worst.  Measured recovery error on this
    // implementation is quoted per line.
    // =======================================================================

    // k = 0 (ATM).  Oracle: sigma = 0.2449489742783178, call = 9.747674982232057.
    let k0_sig: f64 = svi_implied_vol(0.0, 1.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(k0_sig, 0.2449489742783178, 64.0));
    let k0_px: f64 = bs_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.0, 0.0, k0_sig, 1.0);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(k0_px, 9.747674982232057, 512.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_solve_call(k0_px, 100.0, 100.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0), k0_sig, 64.0));

    // k = -0.2 (in the money).  K = 81.87307530779819.
    // Oracle: sigma = 0.2725514102833847, call = 21.435511790221017.
    let km_sig: f64 = svi_implied_vol(0.0 - 0.2, 1.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(km_sig, 0.2725514102833847, 64.0));
    let km_px: f64 = bs_call(100.0, 81.87307530779819, 0.0, 0.0, km_sig, 1.0);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(km_px, 21.435511790221017, 512.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_solve_call(km_px, 100.0, 81.87307530779819, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0), km_sig, 128.0));

    // k = +0.2 (out of the money).  K = 122.14027581601698.
    // Oracle: sigma = 0.24956816953983113, call = 3.2922608980022843.
    let kp_sig: f64 = svi_implied_vol(0.2, 1.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(kp_sig, 0.24956816953983113, 64.0));
    let kp_px: f64 = bs_call(100.0, 122.14027581601698, 0.0, 0.0, kp_sig, 1.0);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(kp_px, 3.2922608980022843, 512.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(rt_solve_call(kp_px, 100.0, 122.14027581601698, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0), kp_sig, 256.0));

    // The smile is a SMILE: the recovered vols differ across strikes, and the
    // in-the-money (k < 0) vol is the highest — the negative-rho skew the FREE
    // parameters encode.  A flat-vol bug would round-trip every case perfectly
    // and fail only here.
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(km_sig - k0_sig));
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(kp_sig - k0_sig));
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(km_sig - kp_sig));

    // =======================================================================
    // 3. VALUE CHECKS against the 50-digit oracle.
    //
    // w, w', w'' and g at five log-moneyness points on the FREE slice, and g at
    // three points on the VIOL slice.  Bands: w and w' are two or three
    // arithmetic operations from a single sqrt and sit at 256 ulps, the same as
    // the black_scholes anchors.  g is a difference of squares divided by w and
    // carries a genuine cancellation, so it gets 4096 — the same band the
    // higher-order Greeks use, and for the same reason.
    // =======================================================================

    // FREE, k = 0: the vertex-adjacent point where w' is small (-0.03) and w''
    // is at its largest (0.5).
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_total_variance(0.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.06, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_w_prime(0.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.0 - 0.03, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_w_prime2(0.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.5, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_durrleman_g(0.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 1.24619375, 4096.0));

    // FREE, k = -0.25 and k = +0.25.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_total_variance(0.0 - 0.25, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.07951562118716424, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_w_prime(0.0 - 0.25, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.0 - 0.10808688094430303, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_w_prime2(0.0 - 0.25, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.12189171659598522, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_durrleman_g(0.0 - 0.25, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.7125264923848175, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_total_variance(0.25, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.06451562118716424, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_w_prime(0.25, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.04808688094430303, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_durrleman_g(0.25, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.8741832613223867, 4096.0));

    // FREE, k = -0.5 and k = +0.5 — the wings of the quoted range.  Note w is
    // NOT symmetric about k = 0 (0.1089 against 0.0789): the asymmetry is
    // exactly b*rho*2k, and a lost rho would make these two equal.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_total_variance(0.0 - 0.5, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.10885164807134504, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_w_prime(0.0 - 0.5, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.0 - 0.12284766908852593, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_w_prime2(0.0 - 0.5, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.025613150093386463, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_durrleman_g(0.0 - 0.5, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.4925187420907028, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_total_variance(0.5, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.07885164807134504, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_w_prime(0.5, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.06284766908852593, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_durrleman_g(0.5, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.6412225429860161, 4096.0));

    // VIOL, the three negative-g points.  These are value checks on the
    // MAGNITUDE of the violation, not just its sign: a g that was negative for
    // the wrong reason (say, a dropped w''/2 term) would have the right sign
    // and the wrong number, and only these lines would notice.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_durrleman_g(0.0, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs), 0.0 - 1.4061272595161562, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_durrleman_g(0.0 - 0.25, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs), 0.0 - 0.7872340678453167, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_durrleman_g(0.0 - 0.5, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs), 0.0 - 0.4475327977402055, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_total_variance(0.0, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs), 0.17812305898749053, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_w_prime(0.0, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs), 0.0 - 1.4799844718999242, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_w_prime2(0.0, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs), 1.6099689437998486, 256.0));

    // Implied vols at three maturities on the FREE slice.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_implied_vol(0.0 - 0.3, 0.5, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.41244517879262427, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_implied_vol(0.0, 1.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.2449489742783178, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_implied_vol(0.3, 2.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.18310586112224794, 256.0));

    // =======================================================================
    // 4. DOMAIN: every inadmissible parameter set returns the canonical qNaN.
    //
    // 0x7FF8000000000000 exactly, from an explicit bit literal — never a NaN
    // synthesised by 0.0/0.0, whose sign bit differs between x86 and ARM and
    // would break byte-identity on precisely the error path nobody inspects.
    // =======================================================================

    // b < 0: both wings inverted, w unbounded below.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_total_variance(0.0, fa, 0.0 - 0.1, frho, fm, fs)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_w_prime(0.0, fa, 0.0 - 0.1, frho, fm, fs)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_durrleman_g(0.0, fa, 0.0 - 0.1, frho, fm, fs)));
    // |rho| >= 1: Lee's moment boundary, and a divide by zero in k_min.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_total_variance(0.0, fa, fb, 1.0, fm, fs)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_total_variance(0.0, fa, fb, 0.0 - 1.0, fm, fs)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_total_variance(0.0, fa, fb, 1.5, fm, fs)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_k_min(fa, fb, 1.0, fm, fs)));
    // p_sigma <= 0: the hyperbola degenerates to a corner; w'' is a spike.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_total_variance(0.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_total_variance(0.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, 0.0 - 0.2)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_w_prime2(0.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, 0.0)));
    // Negative MINIMUM total variance.  a = -0.05 with b*p_sigma*sqrt(1-rho^2)
    // = 0.1*0.2*0.9539 = 0.01908 leaves a minimum of -0.0309: imaginary vol.
    // Note `a < 0` alone is NOT the test — the next check proves a negative a
    // is accepted when the wings lift the vertex back above zero.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_total_variance(0.0, 0.0 - 0.05, fb, frho, fm, fs)));
    // ... and the positive control for that guard: a = -0.01 gives a minimum of
    // +0.00908, which is admissible.  Without this line, a guard that rejected
    // every negative `a` would pass the check above and be silently wrong.
    fails = fails + is_nan(svi_total_variance(0.0, 0.0 - 0.01, fb, frho, fm, fs));
    fails = fails + expect_true(gt_zero(svi_total_variance(0.0, 0.0 - 0.01, fb, frho, fm, fs)));
    // t <= 0 in the vol conversion.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_implied_vol(0.0, 0.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_implied_vol(0.0, 0.0 - 1.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs)));
    // A NaN anywhere in the inputs propagates; it is never swallowed.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_total_variance(canonical_qnan(), fa, fb, frho, fm, fs)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_total_variance(0.0, canonical_qnan(), fb, frho, fm, fs)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_durrleman_g(0.0, fa, fb, canonical_qnan(), fm, fs)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(svi_implied_vol(0.0, canonical_qnan(), fa, fb, frho, fm, fs)));
    // The scans fail CLOSED on bad parameters: an unevaluatable surface is not
    // a surface shown to be arbitrage-free.
    fails = fails + expect_false(svi_butterfly_ok(0.0 - 0.5, 0.5, 40, fa, 0.0 - 0.1, frho, fm, fs));
    fails = fails + expect_false(svi_butterfly_ok(0.0 - 0.5, 0.5, 0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs));
    fails = fails + expect_false(svi_butterfly_ok(canonical_qnan(), 0.5, 40, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs));
    fails = fails + expect_false(svi_calendar_ok(0.0 - 0.5, 0.5, 40, 1.0, fa, fb, 1.0, fm, fs, 2.0, ga, gb, grho, gm, gs));
    // b = 0 is the flat slice and IS admitted — degenerate but not
    // arbitrageable.  Included so the b guard is known to reject only b < 0.
    fails = fails + is_nan(svi_total_variance(0.3, fa, 0.0, frho, fm, fs));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(svi_total_variance(0.3, fa, 0.0, frho, fm, fs), 0.04, 256.0));

    // =======================================================================
    // 5. ADVERSARIAL NEGATIVE CONTROLS.
    //
    // Each line below adds `chk_ulp(...)` to `fails` POSITIVELY: a return of 1
    // means the band ACCEPTED a value known to be wrong, and that counts as a
    // failure.  Without this block every band above could be wide enough to
    // accept anything and the whole file would be green and worthless.
    //
    // The wrong values are chosen to sit just OUTSIDE the corresponding band,
    // so each is a real test of that band rather than of an absurd number.
    // =======================================================================

    // (a) w at the vertex, wrong in the 10th decimal.  Band is 256 ulps of
    //     0.06, about 3.4e-15; the control is off by 1e-10, roughly 30000x.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(svi_total_variance(0.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.0600000001, 256.0);
    // (b) w' at the vertex with the SIGN flipped.  A sign error in the rho term
    //     is the single likeliest transcription error in this file.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(svi_w_prime(0.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.03, 256.0);
    // (c) g at the vertex, wrong in the 8th decimal.  g carries the widest band
    //     in the file (4096 ulps of 1.246, about 1.1e-12), and a wide band is
    //     exactly where a vacuous pass hides.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(svi_durrleman_g(0.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 1.2461937600000002, 4096.0);
    // (d) The SWAPPED WING control.  b*(1+rho) = 0.07 must not be accepted as
    //     the LEFT wing slope, whose true limit is -0.13.  This is the
    //     adversarial form of the rho-sign claim: a flipped rho leaves w(0)
    //     and w'' untouched and is caught only here.
    fails = fails + chk_abs(svi_w_prime(0.0 - 200.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.0 - 0.07, 0.000001);
    fails = fails + chk_abs(svi_w_prime(200.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.13, 0.000001);
    // (e) A DROPPED-w''-TERM control, stated as a value.  g(0) without the
    //     +w''/2 term would be 1.24619375 - 0.25 = 0.99619375.  The band must
    //     reject it; if it did not, the mutation test on this file could not
    //     distinguish a correct g from a convex-but-conservative one.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(svi_durrleman_g(0.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.99619375, 4096.0);
    // (f) A DROPPED-DESTABILISER control.  g(0) without the -(w'^2/4)(1/w+1/4)
    //     term would be 1.24619375 + 0.00376875 = 1.2499625.  This is the
    //     SILENT mutation — the one that makes every surface look clean — so
    //     it gets its own control.  The oracle value of the dropped term at
    //     k = 0 is (0.03^2/4)*(1/0.06 + 0.25) = 0.00376875 exactly.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(svi_durrleman_g(0.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 1.2499625, 4096.0);
    // (g) A FLAT-VOL control: the surface's ATM vol must not be accepted as its
    //     k = -0.2 vol.  A smile collapsed to a single number passes every
    //     round-trip in section 2 and fails only this.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(svi_implied_vol(0.0 - 0.2, 1.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.2449489742783178, 64.0);
    // (h) A WRONG-MATURITY control: the T=1 vol must not be accepted as the
    //     T=2 vol.  Catches a dropped or inverted 1/t in the conversion, which
    //     the iv^2*t == w consistency check would NOT catch if t were dropped
    //     from both sides.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(svi_implied_vol(0.0, 2.0, fa, fb, frho, fm, fs), 0.2449489742783178, 256.0);
    // (i) A WRONG-PARAMETER-SET control: the VIOL slice's w at the vertex must
    //     not be accepted as the FREE slice's.  Proves the parameters are
    //     actually reaching the arithmetic rather than being ignored in favour
    //     of a hard-coded slice.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(svi_total_variance(0.0, va, vb, vrho, vm, vs), 0.06, 256.0);
    // (j) A FLUSH-TO-ZERO control on the minimum: w'(k_min) is asserted against
    //     an absolute 1e-13 band, so confirm that band rejects a value that is
    //     merely small rather than zero.
    fails = fails + chk_abs(0.0000001, 0.0, 0.0000000000001);

    return fails;
}
